This talks about how hash tables are formed. Roughly there are two ways viz.,
-> Chaining
-> Open Addressing

NOTE:
-> "key" here refers to the key obtained after compression map
-> load factor is #elements / #slots ... lesser the better!!

<a> Chaining
Key gives us the index. At the index there is a pointer to another data structure where data is stored. Handles
collisions well. Now you could have a linkedlist or a AVL tree (like Solaris HAT) its upto you. 

Essentially data is stored outside the hash table.


<b> Open Addressing
Use key to search index if not present search other locations in the hash table.

--> Linear Probing: Continue searching subsequent locations until match found or empty or wrapped around.

--> Double Hashing: Use two hash functions first one for index second one for offsets by which to increment
    to do the linear search. This prevents long searches and if the second key is different for different elements
    allows uniform distribution.

    Caveat: Trick mentioned by Naveen Garg, use a prime number between 0 ~ m-1 for the second key. Why? because after
    wrapping it will allow you to search elements that were not initially searched. So in the worst case for associativity
    it acts like a fully set associative map.

